=== entry 651cc2734134
kind: policy
rules: delegate, store, pr-wait
intent: Translation: workers are spawned with `delegate_task` (whole kickoff in `task`, one `clientRequestId` per spawn, keep `taskId` and `childThreadId`), the program's state lives in `notes.md` in the shared pstack store, and PRs are watched with `watch_pull_request` (with the merge heartbeat and unwatch from rule pr-wait); upstream's "when your harness has them" is dropped since T3 always has it. Policy (change reload-the-orchestrator-after): names `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` as the one file that survives compaction, rewritten under 6,000 characters with the standing orders, live agents and their `taskId`s, open PRs, open gates, and next steps, and printed back by the compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` once it is installed in the project's `.claude/settings.json`. It still applies while that change ships the reference and the frontmatter hook. Preserve "Call no scripts, run no polling loops, never relay".
--- upstream
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `Task`. Keep the program's state in the Project tracker (`notes.md`) and the Project store. Watch each PR with PR and CI event subscriptions when your harness has them. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.
--- patched
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `delegate_task`, with the whole kickoff in `task` and its own `clientRequestId` for each spawn, and keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. Keep the program's state in `notes.md` in the store, `~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"`. Create it if missing. `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` is the one file that survives compaction, so it lives in the project and not the store. Rewrite it whenever the program's state changes, under 6,000 characters. It holds the standing orders, the live agents with their `taskId`s, the open PRs, the open gates, and the next steps. The compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints it back; install that hook in the project's `.claude/settings.json`, or the file is never read. Watch each PR with `watch_pull_request`, then end the turn. It wakes on a failed check, required checks passing, another person's comment or review, or a new conflict. A merge ends the watch without a wake, so pair it with a `schedule_task` heartbeat while a PR waits to merge, and call `unwatch_pull_request` once the PR is done. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.

=== entry ca155dd357b3
kind: translation
rules: isolation, verify-surface, team-kit, cursor-only, model-effort, resume, stop, same-model-review
intent: Workers default to this machine: a writer gets its own worktree (commit first or name a pushed branch), a reader shares the checkout, and the list of reasons to stay off a cloud VM becomes a note that every child runs here, with runtime checks through the project's verification skill, `preview_*` tools, or the shell. Effort is set under the role line's ceiling (rule model-effort's fixed sentence). T3 has no fork, so new work that needs a worker's built state goes to that worker with `t3_thread_send` (closest faithful version, no rule covers fork), and a check-in reads `task_status` instead of messaging. Policy: the verifier runs on the same model and effort as the worker it checks, unless the user named another (rule same-model-review). Preserve one owner per PR, the three-level cap, the rolling window of about ten, and starting with one unit.
--- upstream
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run workers on a new cloud VM (`environment: "cloud"`) unless the work needs this machine: a `control-ui` or `control-cli` runtime check (from `cursor-team-kit`), local transcripts, a simulator, or auth that exists only here. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on a different model family. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
--- patched
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. A worker that writes gets `isolation: "worktree"`, a checkout of your current local commit, so commit first, or name a pushed branch for it to check out. A worker that only reads gets no `isolation` and shares your checkout, with scratch copies in `/tmp`. Every child runs on this machine, so a runtime check (the project's own verification skill, T3's `preview_*` tools, or the shell for a CLI), transcripts, a simulator, and auth that exists only here work from any of them. Set each subagent's effort for the job per the Subagents section, lowering its line's effort for routine work and never raising it.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Hand new work that needs a worker's built state to that worker,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process, with `t3_thread_send` to its `childThreadId`, since a fresh worker starts from a commit and sees none of it. Otherwise message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent. Read its `task_status` instead.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on the same model and effort as the worker it checks, unless the user named another. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
